<h1>Video 100 — Inner Classes Part-1 Introduction</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java With OCJP/SCJP: Innerclass  Part- 1||Introduction</p>
<p>ASR decode: keep subing / keep supporting = keep writing; n class / in our / NR = inner; GOI / GI / gii / aw GI = GUI; jit = JIT; whoops / easier = has-a / IS-A; Ned / Nest / inad = nested; meod local = method-local; anonimous = anonymous; dollar = $; outter = Outer; No Such method error colon mean = NoSuchMethodError: main; legend / Len = listen; injp / cjp / scjp = OCJP/SCJP; Yan entry = an entry.</p>
<h3>00:05 — Inner classes: hard topic (with generics)</h3>
<p>Next topic: inner classes — a bit difficult in total SCJP/OCJP. Only two “difficult” topics at core Java / OCJP level: generics and inner classes. Take special care (new syntax). Relatively simpler than generics. Board: Inner classes.</p>
<h3>00:52 — Definition</h3>
<p>A class declared inside another class is an inner class.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    class A {
        // A is inner class of Test
    }
}
// Board:
// Sometimes we can declare a class inside another class
// Such type of classes are called inner classes</pre>
<h3>02:21 — History: why 1.1 introduced inner classes</h3>
<p>Java 1.0 (1995): Sun hyped platform-independent, OOP, robust, secured, simple language. Experts happy, but found two weak areas:</p>
<ol><li>Performance not up to mark vs C/C++.</li></ol>
<ol><li>GUI concepts had many bugs.</li></ol>
<p>Java 1.1 came in ~3 months targeting those:</p>
<ul><li>Performance → JIT (Just-In-Time) compilers — he jokes improvement was tiny (0.00001%); performance still Java’s biggest pain even through 1.8 / “today.”</li></ul>
<ul><li>GUI bugs → event handling / listeners.</li></ul>
<p>As part of event handling, Sun first used class inside class → inner classes introduced in 1.1 to fix GUI bugs. Because of powerful features, programmers later used inner classes in regular coding too (not only GUI).</p>
<pre class='code'>// Board:
// Inner classes concept introduced in 1.1 version
// to fix GUI bug as a part of event handling
// but because of powerful features and benefits of inner classes
// slowly programmers have started using in regular coding also</pre>
<h3>09:34 — When to use inner classes (design rule)</h3>
<p>If parent–child reuse → inheritance. For inner classes:</p>
<p>Without existing one type of object, if there is no chance of existing another type of object → go for inner classes.</p>
<p>Remember the line; three examples make it clear.</p>
<pre class='code'>// Board:
// Without existing one type of object
// if there is no chance of existing another type of object
// then we should go for inner classes</pre>
<h3>12:21 — Example 1: University and Department</h3>
<p>University has several departments (CS, Electronics, Electrical…). If government closes the university → all departments close. Department is always part of University — cannot exist alone.</p>
<pre class='code'>class University {
    class Department {
        // Department is always part of University
    }
}
// University = outer class; Department = inner class
// Board Example 1:
// University consists of several departments
// Without existing University there is no chance of existing Department
// Hence we have to declare Department class inside University class</pre>
<h3>16:37 — Example 2: Car and Engine</h3>
<p>Engine is major component of Car. Without Car object, no Engine object. Engine always part of Car.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Car {
    class Engine {
        // Engine is always part of Car
    }
}
// Car = outer; Engine = inner
// Board Example 2:
// Without existing Car object there is no chance of existing Engine object
// Hence we have to declare Engine class inside Car class</pre>
<h3>19:26 — Example 3: Map and Entry (from Java API)</h3>
<p>Map = group of key–value pairs; each pair = an Entry. Without Map object, no Entry object. So `interface Entry` is defined inside `interface Map`.</p>
<p>interface Map {
    interface Entry {
        // Entry is always part of Map
    }
}
// Map = outer interface; Entry = inner interface</p>
<pre class='code'>// Board Example 3:
// Map is a group of key value pairs
// and each key value pair is called an Entry
// Without existing Map object there is no chance of existing Entry object
// Hence interface Entry is defined inside Map interface</pre>
<h3>24:07 — Note 1 from examples</h3>
<pre class='code'>// Note 1:
// Without existing outer class object
// there is no chance of existing inner class object</pre>
<h3>25:50 — Note 2: relation is has-a, not IS-A</h3>
<p>Is outer parent and inner child? No. University has-a Department; Car has-a Engine; Map has-a Entry.</p>
<pre class='code'>// Board:
// The relation between outer class and inner class
// is not IS-A relation
// and it is has-a relationship
// (composition or aggregation)</pre>
<h3>27:58 — Four types of inner classes</h3>
<p>Based on position of declaration and behavior, four types:</p>
<ol><li>Normal or regular inner classes</li></ol>
<ol><li>Method-local inner classes</li></ol>
<ol><li>Anonymous inner classes</li></ol>
<ol><li>Static nested classes</li></ol>
<pre class='code'>// Board:
// Based on position of declaration and behavior
// all inner classes are divided into four types
// 1. Normal or regular inner classes
// 2. Method local inner classes
// 3. Anonymous inner classes
// 4. Static nested classes</pre>
<p>Why “nested” not “inner” for type 4? Big reason later when teaching static nested classes — not namesake.</p>
<h3>32:49 — Normal / regular inner class definition</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Outer {
    class Inner {
    }
}</pre>
<p>Is this static nested? No (no static). Anonymous? No (has name). Method-local? No (not inside a method). Remaining → normal or regular inner class.</p>
<p>Interview wording (don’t define by “not the other three” circularly — say positively):</p>
<pre class='code'>// Board:
// If we are declaring any named class
// directly inside a class
// without static modifier
// such type of inner class is called
// normal or regular inner class</pre>
<h3>37:07 — Compiling Outer.java → two .class files</h3>
<p>Saved as Outer.java. Compile → two .class files (one per class):</p>
<ul><li>Outer → Outer.class</li></ul>
<ul><li>Inner → `Outer$Inner.class` (dollar = inner-class marker)</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>class Outer {
    class Inner {
    }
}
// javac Outer.java
// produces: Outer.class  and  Outer$Inner.class</pre>
<p>In JAR listings, $ in a class file name → clear indication of inner class. Before $ = outer name; after = inner name.</p>
<h3>39:25 — Running without main → NoSuchMethodError</h3>
<p>Neither Outer nor Inner has main:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Outer {
    class Inner {
    }
}</pre>
<p># java Outer
# Exception in thread &quot;main&quot; java.lang.NoSuchMethodError: main

# java Outer$Inner
# Exception in thread &quot;main&quot; java.lang.NoSuchMethodError: main</p>
<p>(He also demos with JDK 1.6 path; compiles fine; same runtime errors.)</p>
<h3>43:24 — Example 1 on board (compile + run outcomes)</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Outer {
    class Inner {
    }
}
// Normal or regular inner class
// javac Outer.java → Outer.class , Outer$Inner.class
// java Outer        → NoSuchMethodError: main
// java Outer$Inner  → NoSuchMethodError: main</pre>
<h3>45:53 — Example 2: main inside Outer</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Outer {
    class Inner {
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(&quot;Outer class main method&quot;);
        // prints Outer class main method
    }
}</pre>
<p># javac Outer.java → two class files
# java Outer       → prints Outer class main method
# java Outer$Inner → NoSuchMethodError: main  (Inner has no main)</p>
<h3>50:36 — Example 3: main inside Inner → CE</h3>
<p>Without outer object, inner cannot exist → inner is instance related → static members not allowed inside normal inner class (including main).</p>
<p>Many students expect: compile OK, java Outer$Inner prints. Reality: compile-time error.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Outer {
    class Inner {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            System.out.println(&quot;Inner class main method&quot;);
            // CE: inner classes cannot have static declarations
        }
    }
}
// Board:
// Inside inner class we can&#x27;t declare any static members
// hence we can&#x27;t declare main method
// and we can&#x27;t run inner class directly from command prompt</pre>
<p>Compiler message he stresses: inner classes cannot have static declarations.</p>
<h3>58:02 — Instance method inside Inner is OK</h3>
<p>Static not allowed, but instance methods are fine — otherwise no point to the class.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Outer {
    class Inner {
        public void m1() {
            System.out.println(&quot;Inner class method&quot;);
            // prints Inner class method  (when called via inner object)
        }
    }
}</pre>
<h3>59:23 — Case 1: access inner code from **static** area of Outer</h3>
<p>To call m1, need Inner object; without Outer object, no Inner object.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Outer {
    class Inner {
        public void m1() {
            System.out.println(&quot;Inner class method&quot;);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Outer o = new Outer();
        Outer.Inner i = o.new Inner();
        i.m1();
        // prints Inner class method

        // combine two lines:
        Outer.Inner i2 = new Outer().new Inner();
        i2.m1();

        // combine three lines (create + call):
        new Outer().new Inner().m1();
        // prints Inner class method
    }
}</pre>
<p>Note: .class file uses Outer$Inner, but in source refer as `Outer.Inner`. Creation: `outerRef.new Inner()`.</p>
<pre class='code'>// Case 1 — Accessing inner class code from static area of outer class</pre>
<h3>1:07:09 — Case 2: access from **instance** area of Outer (easier)</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Outer {
    class Inner {
        public void m1() {
            System.out.println(&quot;Inner class method&quot;);
        }
    }

    public void m2() {
        Inner i = new Inner(); // outer object already exists (this)
        i.m1();
        // prints Inner class method
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Outer o = new Outer();
        o.m2();
    }
}</pre>
<p>Why new Inner() works in m2 without o.new? Entering m2 already required an Outer object — that outer exists as this.</p>
<p>Instance area access is easier than static area (normal syntax, less “new Outer().new Inner()”).</p>
<pre class='code'>// Case 2 — Accessing inner class code from instance area of outer class</pre>
<p>OCJP may ask: from line of m2, which code properly calls m1?</p>
<h3>1:13:29 — Case 3: access from **outside** Outer (same as Case 1)</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Outer {
    class Inner {
        public void m1() {
            System.out.println(&quot;Inner class method&quot;);
        }
    }
}

class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Outer o = new Outer();
        Outer.Inner i = o.new Inner();
        i.m1();
        // prints Inner class method

        // or:
        new Outer().new Inner().m1();
    }
}</pre>
<p>Code same as accessing from static area of Outer.</p>
<pre class='code'>// Case 3 — Accessing inner class code from outside of outer class</pre>
<h3>1:17:47 — Summary of three cases</h3>
<pre class='code'>// Accessing inner class code:

// From static area of outer class OR from outside of outer class:
Outer o = new Outer();
Outer.Inner i = o.new Inner();
i.m1();
// or: new Outer().new Inner().m1();

// From instance area of outer class:
Inner i = new Inner();
i.m1();</pre>
<p>Must be familiar with both styles for OCJP.</p>
